// The `standard:` tier — OPERATOR THEORY on the Hardy-Sobolev scale.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Theorem 11.3.2.1 (Orbital Traces on the Paley-Wiener Levels) was a
// single bare hole, justified as "it IS the analysis, and decomposing it would only mint
// opaque predicates". That was half right: PROVING it needs Hardy-Sobolev spaces,
// nuclearity and trace-class operators, which this formalization does not have. But its
// proof NAMES those objects and uses SPECIFIC properties of them, and naming them is not
// the same as proving anything about them.
//
// So this file declares the interface the manuscript's proof reaches for, and cites the
// classical facts it invokes. That turns one opaque assumption into a located vocabulary
// plus a small number of named inputs — each of which a reader can check against a textbook
// or against the manuscript, and each of which can be formalized later on its own schedule.
//
// ALTITUDE: opaque, like the rest. `NuclearOperator` carries no topology; `trace` is a
// symbol with a defining property, not a construction. These are the INTERFACES, not the
// theorems.

theory vocab <<< "../shared/vocabulary.b4m"

sort Quantity = vocab.Quantity
sort TestFunction = vocab.TestFunction

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the objects 11.3.2.1's proof names
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// the Hardy-Sobolev space W_I^s(C_F) in which the manuscript's convergence takes place
/// (§9.2.2, weight w_{I,s}; the Paley-Wiener levels of Ch. 11 sit inside it)
sort HardySobolevSpace

/// a continuous linear operator on a Hardy-Sobolev space
sort Operator

/// a kernel function on C_F x C_F inducing an operator by integration
sort Kernel

// NOTE: `sort Cutoff` and `sort StratumIndicator` were declared here and never used. The
// manuscript's rho and alpha_k are real objects, but nothing in this encoding references
// them, so declaring them was DECORATION — vocabulary that made the file look as though it
// modelled the cutoff machinery while no formula mentioned it. Removed; add them back when
// a statement actually needs them.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the properties it uses
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// the operator is NUCLEAR (trace-class) on the given space: it has a well-defined trace.
/// The manuscript establishes nuclearity for its own operators in Thms 7.4.2.1, 10.3.2.1,
/// 11.2.2.1 — those are the author's, not classical, and are cited as such below.
pred isNuclearOn(o: Operator, w: HardySobolevSpace)

/// the operator is the limit, in the nuclear (trace) norm, of a sequence of FINITE RANK
/// operators. This is the route 11.3.2.1 takes to its trace: "the same method used in the
/// estimates from Theorem 10.3.2.1 shows that such operator is the limit of the finite rank
/// kernels from (11.2.2.12)".
pred isTheNuclearLimitOfFiniteRanks(o: Operator, w: HardySobolevSpace)

/// the kernel induces a continuous integral operator valued in the range of M_rho
pred inducesAContinuousOperator(k: Kernel, w: HardySobolevSpace)

/// the sequence of cutoff-and-indicator truncations converges in the Hardy-Sobolev space.
/// This is what Theorem 11.1.2.1 (S-local carvings) supplies for the manuscript's kernels.
pred truncationsConvergeIn(k: Kernel, w: HardySobolevSpace)

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// the trace, as a symbol with properties
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// the trace of a nuclear operator. GUARDED: a trace is defined only for a nuclear
/// operator, so every application owes nuclearity at the call site. (std's no-totalization
/// rule: a total `trace` would let a proof name the trace of a non-trace-class operator and
/// discharge obligations about it.)
oper trace(o: Operator, w: HardySobolevSpace) => Quantity requires isNuclearOn(o, w)

/// the operator a kernel induces by integration against the Haar measure
oper operatorOf(k: Kernel, w: HardySobolevSpace) => Operator
  requires inducesAContinuousOperator(k, w)

/// the value of the manuscript's orbital integral — the left side of (11.3.2.1)
oper theOrbitalIntegral(k: Kernel, f: TestFunction) => Quantity

intheory nuclearLimitsAreNuclear

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// CLASSICAL — cited, with sources.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The trace class is CLOSED in its own norm: a trace-norm limit of finite-rank operators
/// is itself nuclear. This is what the chain needs in order to name `trace` at all (the
/// `requires isNuclearOn` guard), and it is all this formula says.
///
/// NOTE ON AN EARLIER OVERCLAIM. This hole was named `theTraceIsContinuousOnTheTraceClass`
/// and cited Reed-Simon Thm VI.26 (continuity of the trace FUNCTIONAL). An adversarial audit
/// pointed out the formula does not mention `trace` at all, so the citation described a
/// theorem the statement does not make. Renamed and re-cited to what it actually asserts.
/// Continuity of the trace functional is a DIFFERENT fact, and the chain does not use it:
/// 11.3.2.1's evaluation of the limit value is `theLimitValueIsTheZeroSide`, a separate
/// bare hole citing the author's Thm 7.4.2.1.
hole theTraceClassIsNormClosed
  cites "standard: the trace class S_1 is a Banach space in the trace norm, hence closed under trace-norm limits; the finite-rank operators are dense in it — Reed-Simon, Functional Analysis I, Thm VI.26 and the surrounding discussion of S_1; Simon, Trace Ideals and Their Applications §1-2; Conway, A Course in Operator Theory Ch.3 §18 (which the manuscript itself cites at (10.4.2.25) for the S1 = S2 identity)":
  forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
  isTheNuclearLimitOfFiniteRanks(o, w) -> isNuclearOn(o, w)

/// A finite-rank limit is nuclear, so `trace` may be named at it.
theorem nuclearLimitsAreNuclear:
  forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
  isTheNuclearLimitOfFiniteRanks(o, w) -> isNuclearOn(o, w)
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall o: Operator; forall w: HardySobolevSpace;
      isTheNuclearLimitOfFiniteRanks(o, w) -> isNuclearOn(o, w)
    [by cite theTraceClassIsNormClosed]
qed
